{"author":{"id":"3f8bad4585cb0f07c6570d9e31f06264af3b0abf48efbc5a44f56df5e7a9cd45"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1789460320,"nonce":"dvgbcPF6k5jufKZoNAullkE2ZQo=","title":"One world: Wren's fibers and threads as core tasks","message":"wren_lift's scheduler (sched.rs: worlds, tasks, wait tokens, endpoints, timers, a pool) and the core's (caribou::sched) are the same design over krio; today a program under caribou runs two of them side by side. A Wren `Fiber.spawn`, `Fiber.sleep`, `Lock.wait`, `Channel.receive` or `Thread.create` parks on wren_lift's world, which drives only Wren tasks while it waits (`Sched::idle`, `drive_until`), and a Haxe `Thread` waits on the core's, which drives only Haxe's. Neither sees the other's tasks: a Wren fiber waiting on a lock a Haxe thread releases holds its OS thread's other tasks, and the reverse. And a Wren task has no host state on the core, so Ash's trap chain is not swapped around it: a Haxe call from a Wren task that parks leaks its traps into whoever runs next (the fiber gap noted on 1069794).\n\nDesign: the core's world is the one world; wren_lift's scheduler front stays and its world goes behind the runtime seam.\n\nwren_lift (RT_VERSION 4, a World section of slots, each taking the view `vm`; defaults are wren_lift's own Sched):\n- `world_waiter_new(vm) -\u003e token`, `world_waiter_discard(vm, token)`, `world_wake(token) -\u003e bool`, `world_waiter_ready(vm, token) -\u003e i32` (1: woken before the park, consumed; 0: waiting; -1: not this world's).\n- `world_park_request(vm, token, deadline_ns)` records the stepped task's park; the task then yields through wren_lift's own `try_krio_yield` (its JIT-root handoff must run), and the host parks the task when its step returns: the core's stackless protocol (`request_park`, `Pending`, `resume_cause`). `world_park_pending(vm) -\u003e bool` lets `try_krio_call` pass a park through an intermediate fiber. `world_resume_woken(vm) -\u003e bool` after the resume.\n- `world_park_drive(vm, token, deadline_ns) -\u003e bool`: the park of a stack that is no task; the host drives its world meanwhile.\n- `world_spawn(vm, task, on_pool) -\u003e id`: `task` is a context wren_lift made (shared program, closure, handle, fiber once made); the host steps it by calling back `task_step(task) -\u003e bool` (a wren_lift slot read back with `wlift_rt_task_step`), false once the task is done and the context released. `task_step` finds or makes this thread's view of the program (a host's pool worker has none until then), makes the fiber on first step (a fiber runs on the thread that made it), and runs one step as `Sched::resume` does today. Contexts not yet stepped are roots (`Shared.transit`), as closures on their way to a pool worker are.\n- `world_on_task(vm) -\u003e bool`, `world_current(vm) -\u003e id`, `world_tick(vm, deadline_ns) -\u003e bool`, `world_idle(vm, deadline_ns)`, `world_live(vm) -\u003e usize`.\nDeadlines cross as nanoseconds from now, `u64::MAX` for none. `Isolate` is unchanged: its thread gets a world of the host's on demand.\n\ncore (caribou::sched): `wake_token`, `waiter_for`, `notified_before_park`, `discard` (by token, for a runtime that carries only the token); a task's registration finished when the world wakes it (the stackless protocol leaked it); `park_pending`; `spawn_on_pool(Box\u003cdyn Task + Send\u003e)` for a stackless task; `set_task_host_hook(fn(TaskId))`, run on the task's world before its first resume, so an adapter attaches host state to a task another language spawned (Ash: `ExcHost` with no context, as for the main context). That closes the park half of the fiber gap; the other half, a Wren fiber yielding to its caller fiber with Haxe frames below (`Fiber.call`/`yield` inside one task), needs the trap chain per krio stack and is left open here.\n\ncaribou-wren: fills the World slots with the core's; `WrenTask` is a core `Task` whose step is `task_step`. Its pool is the core's, so `Thread.create` places on the core's least-loaded world.\n\nFollow-up, not here: `Thread.current`/a Wren `Thread` handle and Haxe's `sys.thread.Thread` naming the same core task (the handle carries the task id; both classes can be built from one).","files":null}]}